<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigebert III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/630">630</a>–<a href="/years/656">656</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Sigebert III of Austrasia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigebert III was the Merovingian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a>, from 633 or 634 until his death. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a>, he came to the throne as a child, and real power lay with his mayors of the palace, above all Grimoald. In 641 his army was defeated by the rebel duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radulf-of-thuringia">Radulf of Thuringia</a>. Remembered for his piety and grants to monasteries, he was later venerated as a saint.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigebert was born around 630, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a>, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and his concubine Ragnetrude. Dagobert recalled Saint Amand, whom he had banished for criticising his vices, and asked him to baptise the newborn. The ceremony took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, with Charibert II, king of Aquitaine, as godfather. Dagobert entrusted the child's education to Pepin of Landen, who had been mayor of the palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> under Chlotar II until 629, and Pepin took him to his domains in Aquitaine for three years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 633 a revolt of the nobles forced Dagobert to make the three-year-old Sigebert king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, as Chlotar II had once made Dagobert. Dagobert refused to give power to Pepin. He placed Sigebert under the tutelage of Adalgisel as mayor of the palace and of Saint Cunibert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, as regent, and kept Pepin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> as a hostage. When Dagobert died in 639 the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a> became independent again under Sigebert and his half-brother Clovis II, then about eleven and five years old.</p><p>Under Sigebert the mayor of the palace began to hold the chief place in Austrasian politics, and he has been called the first roi fainéant, or do-nothing king, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a>. Pepin replaced Adalgisel in 639 and died the following year, and his son Grimoald succeeded him. When the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> rebelled, in the only war of the reign, Grimoald let the young king head the army, which was defeated by Duke Radulph. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> records that the rout left Sigebert weeping in his saddle. Sigebert lived as a pious adult and used his wealth to found monasteries, hospitals and churches, among them Stavelot-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> and the Abbey of Saint Martin near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sigebert died of natural causes on 1 February 656, aged about 25, and was buried at Saint Martin. His body was found incorrupt in 1063 and moved beside the altar; after the abbey was demolished in 1552 the relics went to Nancy Cathedral. He is venerated as a saint, with his feast on 1 February, and is patron saint of Nancy.</p><p>Grimoald had persuaded the childless king to adopt his son Childebert the Adopted, but Sigebert then had a son, Dagobert II. After Sigebert's death Grimoald had the seven-year-old Dagobert tonsured and sent to a monastery in Ireland and proclaimed Childebert king. Childebert was deposed after seven months and both he and Grimoald were killed. Dagobert II was recalled from Ireland in 676 and took his father's throne after about twenty years in exile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigebert III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Radulf later defeated Dagobert's son <b>Sigebert III</b> in 641 and ruled as a near-independent king.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 648 to 651 the missionary Remaclus founded twin abbeys at Stavelot and Malmedy in the Ardennes forest, on land granted by <b>Sigebert III of Austrasia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-stavelot-malmedy">Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy</a></li></ul></section>
